Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Create a temporary array.
- Compare elements from nums1 and nums2 and store the smaller one in temp.
- Copy remaining elements from either array.
- Finally, copy temp back to nums1.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize an empty temp array.
2. Use two pointers i (nums1) and j (nums2).
3. Compare nums1[i] and nums2[j], push smaller into temp.
4. Copy remaining elements.
5. Copy temp back into nums1.

Time Complexity: O(m + n)
Space Complexity: O(m + n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
        vector<int> temp;
        int i = 0, j = 0;

        while (i < m && j < n) {
            if (nums1[i] <= nums2[j])
                temp.push_back(nums1[i++]);
            else
                temp.push_back(nums2[j++]);
        }

        while (i < m)
            temp.push_back(nums1[i++]);

        while (j < n)
            temp.push_back(nums2[j++]);

        for (int k = 0; k < m + n; k++)
            nums1[k] = temp[k];
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Copy all elements of nums2 into the empty positions of nums1.
- Sort the entire nums1 array.

Algorithm:
1. Insert nums2 into nums1 starting from index m.
2. Sort nums1.

Time Complexity: O((m + n) log(m + n))
Space Complexity: O(1) (Ignoring sorting stack space)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            nums1[m + i] = nums2[i];

        sort(nums1.begin(), nums1.end());
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
Idea:
- Since nums1 has extra space at the end, fill it from the back.
- Compare the largest elements of both arrays.
- Place the larger element at the last available position.
- This avoids shifting elements.

Algorithm:
1. Set:
   - i = m - 1 (last valid element in nums1)
   - j = n - 1 (last element in nums2)
   - k = m + n - 1 (last position in nums1)
2. Compare nums1[i] and nums2[j].
3. Place the larger at nums1[k].
4. Move the corresponding pointer backward.
5. If nums2 still has elements left, copy them.

Time Complexity: O(m + n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void merge(vector<int>& nums1, int m, vector<int>& nums2, int n) {
        int i = m - 1;
        int j = n - 1;
        int k = m + n - 1;

        while (i >= 0 && j >= 0) {
            if (nums1[i] > nums2[j])
                nums1[k--] = nums1[i--];
            else
                nums1[k--] = nums2[j--];
        }

        while (j >= 0)
            nums1[k--] = nums2[j--];
    }
};
```

Interview Notes
---------------
- Brute uses an extra array, making it simple but not space-efficient.
- Sorting after appending is easy to implement but slower than linear merge.
- The optimal approach leverages the extra space at the end of nums1.
- Filling from the back prevents overwriting valid elements.
- If nums2 becomes empty first, nums1 is already correctly placed.
- If nums1 becomes empty first, copy the remaining nums2 elements.
- This is the most commonly expected interview solution.
